  "discussions_data": [
    {
      "id": "4bb968526bca90bf76651cb4a2da7830705f7d85",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1569108244,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> We assume the timezone will not change during the process lifetime, or if it does it can be safely ignored, with some time values in the old timezone perhaps.\n\nE.g., macOS, where if the timezone changes (as it can and does for laptops, for example), all processes are notified and, for all processes in which TZ hasn't been explicitly set in a process, that process picks up the new value and starts converting times with the new value, without additional calls to `tzset()` being necessary.\n\n(Dear autocorrect: there is no routine in macOS named `taser()`.  Thank you very much. :-))",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
